#. type: Plain text
#: archlinux debian-unstable fedora-rawhide
msgid "I<CompressionLevel=>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: archlinux debian-unstable fedora-rawhide
msgid ""
"Specifies the compression level to use for the filesystem configured with "
"I<Format=>\\&. Takes a single argument specifying the compression level to "
"use for the configured compression algorithm\\&. The possible compression "
"levels and their meaning are filesystem specific (refer to the "
"filesystem\\*(Aqs documentation for the exact meaning of a particular "
"compression level)\\&."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: archlinux debian-unstable fedora-rawhide
msgid ""
"Note that this setting is only taken into account when the filesystem "
"configured with I<Format=> supports compression and the I<Compression=> "
"setting is configured explicitly\\&."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: archlinux debian-unstable fedora-rawhide
msgid "I<SupplementFor=>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: archlinux debian-unstable fedora-rawhide
msgid ""
"Takes a partition definition name, such as \"10-esp\"\\&. If specified, "
"B<systemd-repart> will avoid creating this partition and instead prefer to "
"partially merge the two definitions\\&. However, depending on the existing "
"layout of partitions on disk, B<systemd-repart> may be forced to fall back "
"onto un-merging the definitions and using them as originally written, "
"potentially creating this partition\\&. Specifically, B<systemd-repart> will "
"fall back if this partition is found to already exist on disk, or if the "
"target partition already exists on disk but is too small, or if it cannot "
"allocate space for the merged partition for some other reason\\&."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: archlinux debian-unstable fedora-rawhide
msgid ""
"The following fields are merged into the target definition in the specified "
"ways: I<Weight=> and I<PaddingWeight=> are simply overwritten; "
"I<SizeMinBytes=> and I<PaddingMinBytes=> use the larger of the two values; "
"I<SizeMaxBytes=> and I<PaddingMaxBytes=> use the smaller value; and "
"I<CopyFiles=>, I<ExcludeFiles=>, I<ExcludeFilesTarget=>, "
"I<MakeDirectories=>, and I<Subvolumes=> are concatenated\\&."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: archlinux debian-unstable fedora-rawhide
msgid ""
"Usage of this option in combination with I<CopyBlocks=>, I<Encrypt=>, or "
"I<Verity=> is not supported\\&. The target definition cannot set these "
"settings either\\&. A definition cannot simultaneously be a supplement and "
"act as a target for some other supplement definition\\&. A target cannot "
"have more than one supplement partition associated with it\\&."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: archlinux debian-unstable fedora-rawhide
msgid ""
"For example, distributions can use this to implement I<$BOOT> as defined in "
"the \\m[blue]B<Boot Loader Specification>\\m[]\\&\\s-2\\u[2]\\d\\s+2\\&. "
"Distributions may prefer to use the ESP as I<$BOOT> whenever possible, but "
"to adhere to the spec XBOOTLDR must sometimes be used instead\\&. So, they "
"should create two definitions: the first defining an ESP big enough to hold "
"just the bootloader, and a second for the XBOOTLDR that\\*(Aqs sufficiently "
"large to hold kernels and configured as a supplement for the ESP\\&. "
"Whenever possible, B<systemd-repart> will try to merge the two definitions "
"to create one large ESP, but if that\\*(Aqs not allowable due to the "
"existing conditions on disk a small ESP and a large XBOOTLDR will be created "
"instead\\&."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: archlinux debian-unstable fedora-rawhide
msgid ""
"As another example, distributions can also use this to seamlessly share a "
"single /home partition in a multi-boot scenario, while preferring to keep /"
"home on the root partition by default\\&. Having a /home partition separated "
"from the root partition entails some extra complexity: someone has to decide "
"how to split the space between the two partitions\\&. On the other hand, it "
"allows a user to share their home area between multiple installed OSs "
"(i\\&.e\\&. via B<systemd-homed.service >(8))\\&. Distributions should "
"create two definitions: the first for a root partition that takes up some "
"relatively small percentage of the disk, and the second as a supplement for "
"the first to create a /home partition that takes up all the remaining free "
"space\\&. On first boot, if B<systemd-repart> finds an existing /home "
"partition on disk, it\\*(Aqll un-merge the definitions and create just a "
"small root partition\\&. Otherwise, the definitions will be merged and a "
"single large root partition will be created\\&."
msgstr ""

